00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.330 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  this is your Tech news briefing for  Tuesday October 4th I'm Zoe Thomas for  The Wall Street Journal  for more than a year White House  officials have been negotiating with  Executives from Tick Tock they've been  talking about possible measures to  prevent data that the video sharing  company collects on its millions of  American users from being shared with  the Chinese government now Tick Tock  which is owned by the Chinese Tech Giant  bite dance denies that it would share  user information with Beijing but  lawmakers particularly Republicans have  pushed back on that and it's expected  that if the GOP takes control of one or  both chambers of Congress after the  midterms they'll push for hearings on  the issue  sources say that backdrop is adding a  sense of urgency to the White House's  conversations with Tick Tock joining us  to discuss all of this is our reporter  John McKinnon hi John thanks for joining  me hi so what is the Biden  administration's approach to getting a  deal here Ben  the first step the administration took  was to basically Stand Down from some  very aggressive efforts that were  undertaken during the Trump  Administration to either shut down Tick  Tock in the U.S or force it to change  its ownership structure so that it would  be owned by a majority U.S group The  Biden Administration  believed that that was legally  indefensible and thought that there were  probably better solutions to be had and  so they said that they would set out to  reach some conclusions on how to do that  it's taken longer I think than a lot of  people expected for them to come to  those conclusions and reach those deals  although all the signs are that they are  heading towards some agreement  around the end of the year the early  part of next year with Tick Tock and  there's also some indication that  they're going to reach a broader  understanding and issue some orders  about how to deal with the the problem  overall  what kinds of safeguards then should we  expect if a deal is reached with Tick  Tock the administration in what's called  cypheus This is an intergovernmental  group task force that deals with  security issues in  foreign investments in the United States  the indications are that they are going  to require Tick Tock to house the data  that it collects in the U.S and that  they're going to probably Identify some  trusted third-party and likely to be  Oracle to basically monitor the data  flows and monitor how the data is  handled you mentioned that the deal has  taken a little bit longer than some  people expected what have been the  hurdles to getting it done it's pretty  complicated as I understand it to  work out exactly how to achieve these  levels of safety that the US would like  to achieve and it's a very difficult  problem to figure out how to keep this  data safe when the US is having such a  difficult time you know maintaining the  safety of a lot of other data there's  also political considerations that  they've had to deal with some people say  that if they reach a deal before the  midterm elections that might make the  administration and Democrats more  vulnerable politically to these attacks  because it would just be in the public  eye a little bit more so there there's  some technical issues and there are some  perhaps political issues  so that's what you've heard from people  who are familiar with these discussions  what have we heard from Republicans  about these efforts you mentioned  there's political tension there are  certainly some Republicans that are very  much more hawkish on China even than the  administration and would say that Tick  Tock for instance needs to be owned by a  us-based entity which was what the Trump  Administration was trying to achieve and  there are certainly others that say that  the administration needs to make tick  tocks sever all ties with China and not  use any of its algorithms or have any  connection with China and tick tock's  response to all this are they talking  about the deal or about these  accusations  Tick Tock is trying not to comment on  the specifics of these negotiations with  cepheus but they certainly have offered  many assurances that they are not  sharing Americans data with the Chinese  government and Tick Tock is really I  think in a little bit of a difficult  position because I think that they are  perhaps not as trusted by the US  government as they would like to be and  they're frankly not as trusted by the  Chinese government as they would like to  be  unusual is this situation where the U.S  is trying to kind of step in and set up  these safeguards on one hand it's a  little bit unusual because the power of  social media platforms is a new problem  that the US is confronting but on the  other hand the US has had lots of  experience with foreign investment in  the U.S and has had ways of dealing with  National Security risks associated with  foreign investment in the US for a long  time and some of the measures that they  will probably implement including some  of the oversight measures the the board  of overseers that they'll probably  require Tick Tock to create with a lot  of U.S citizens and U.S National  Security Experts on it those are  measures that they've done in the past  with other U.S entities that have been  owned or controlled by foreigners but  again the circumstances here are  somewhat new and unique and very hard to  figure out like how do you protect all  this data this massive amounts of data  that are collected on the internet and  are so easily transferred  all right so what do we expect to happen  next then at some point I think probably  after the midterm elections but maybe  before Congress really gets cranked up  in January they'll probably be an  announcement of a cepheus  National Security agreement with Tick  Tock and at that point Tick Tock will  have some of the concerns of all of this  lifted off their shoulders and we'll go  from there  all right that's our reporter John  McKinnon thanks for joining us John oh  thank you  and that's it for today's Tech news  briefing if you want more Tech stories  check out our website wsj.com I'm Zoe  Thomas for The Wall Street Journal  thanks for listening  [Music]  [Music]

